<p>Let me explain the key variables:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">beginPoint</code> — start point: the center X of the current view offset 5dp to the left (the X axis runs left-right); the Y coordinate is the view’s height, i.e., the very bottom
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">endPoint</code> — end point: the start point’s X minus the 40 offset to the left (i.e., farther from the left edge); Y is also reduced by the offset, moving to the top-left outside of the view.
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">controlPoint</code> — control point: for the start point, e.g., X is 30 - 60/2.0 - 60 = -60, which clearly runs off the far left, beyond the view bounds; the trailing + controlLength for Y means the Y coordinate is increased.</p>
</blockquote>

